  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.2. Increased capacity of Member States to apply international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In October  2023, the Philippine government and the UN signed the new United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UN SDCF) for 2024-2028, with President Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. as witness. The issue of upholding labour rights as human rights where tripartite partners (government, employers, workers) will be supported by the UN on capacity building efforts is tackled under UN SDCF &quot;Outcome 2: By 2028, all people, benefit from a more integrated, innovative, inclusive, and sustainable economy that generates decent work and livelihood opportunities&quot; particularly under &quot;Output 2.4: Institutions and systems are enhanced to provide an enabling, rights-based, inclusive environment to address inequalities and support equal opportunities&quot;.This would  include application of relevant international labour standards. In addition to upholding labour rights, the UN will make significant contributions to achieve Outcome 2 by tackling issues to counter discriminatory practices, violence and harassment at the workplace, and improvement of women&#x2019;s participation, representation, and leadership in the workforce. The ILO facilitated the participation of workers&apos; organizations in the Philippines in the UN Philippines&#x2019; processes through the conduct of a series of meetings to prepare workers&apos; organizations for a more systematic engagement including the formulation of UN SDCF. Moreover, the ILO supported workers&apos; organizations in the preparation of key documents which served as inputs to the UN SDCF formulation. This includes the 15-pt Labor Agenda and the Joint Union Paper on &#x2018;Labor&#x2019;s Review of the SDG Goals and Targets for the 2022 Philippine Voluntary National Review (VNR)&#x2019;.

The ILO also linked workers&apos; and employers &apos;organizations to the National Economic and Development Authority&apos;s (NEDA) Stakeholder&#x2019;s Chamber on the SDGs. This resulted to two social partners, the Employers Confederation of the Philippines (ECOP) and Public Services Labor Independent Confederation (PSLINK), to be included as new members of the Stakeholder&apos;s Chamber.</narrative>
   </description>
